abstract: >
  ThermalShift demonstrates that persistent machine-level thermal differences
  on the Frontier supercomputer can be converted into a workload-placement signal
  beyond what cabinet-level structure alone explains. In a preregistered held-out
  challenge experiment (25 new scenarios, sc30–sc54), fingerprint-guided placement
  produced a median conservative bracket separation of +6.71 pp versus random
  placement (bootstrap 95% CI: [+3.92, +8.72] pp) and +3.54 pp versus a
  within-cabinet permutation placebo (95% CI: [+0.56, +4.44] pp), both excluding
  zero at the preregistered 1.25x choice ratio. A prior held-out evaluation
  (Gate F, sc5–sc29, corrected cabinet model) confirmed a positive advantage at
  every nondegenerate tested ratio (r = 1.25–8.0).
